gm-method/rendering perf: an UNCONDITIONAL gl.getError() in the per-frame render path is a hidden GPU-CPU SYNC STALL -- getError() flushes the GPU pipeline and blocks the CPU until it drains, so it can eat several percent of frame time (live-profiled at ~5.5% on a settled frame). It hides especially in a STATIC-frame fast path (a renderer that caches its draw set when the camera holds still runs that branch every idle frame). FIX: gate the check behind a debug flag (window.__glCheck or equivalent) so diagnostics opt in and production pays nothing; check only on demand or every N frames. Find it by a SETTLED steady-state profile (let the scene fully load + idle ~12s, no camera movement, THEN capture) -- a profile taken while teleporting/streaming buries it under load-cost (texture decode, shader first-use, model LOD). General lesson for a 'take every perf win' sweep against a GPU-bound frame: the real CPU wins are the cheap things done EVERY frame that needn't be (a sync query, a per-frame .setSize on a transient FPS swing, a re-filter), not the inherent-optimal hot loops (a tight n-vertex AABB-frustum BVH cull is already optimal -- shrinking it is a phantom win). Also: when the slow code lives in an installed/published dependency that is ALSO a sibling source checkout, fix BOTH -- the canonical fix in the sibling repo (commit+push there) AND an idempotent marker-gated bridge in the consumer's postinstall patch script so the win survives reinstall until a published version carries it.